WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.817
[MUSIC]

00:00:06.879 --> 00:00:10.569
For your child, there is no cure,

00:00:10.569 --> 00:00:14.778
the words that no parent wants to hear.

00:00:14.778 --> 00:00:20.549
But those were the words the doctor said
about my little nephew, Ian, at age four.

00:00:20.549 --> 00:00:25.526
Ian went suddenly from being his normal,
sunshine,

00:00:25.526 --> 00:00:29.838
energetic self,
to being sick in a hospital,

00:00:29.838 --> 00:00:34.721
holding on to his teddy bear and
holding on for life.

00:00:34.721 --> 00:00:36.613
The doctor said it was very close,

00:00:36.613 --> 00:00:40.410
apparently a lot of kids die because
they're not diagnosed in time.

00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:43.730
And then he said those words,
he said, there is no cure.

00:00:43.730 --> 00:00:49.176
He said, we think we're about
10 to 15 years away from a cure,

00:00:49.176 --> 00:00:52.889
but then again, we said that 15 years ago.

00:00:52.889 --> 00:00:56.835
And I know many of you don't have
to imagine what that feels like

00:00:56.835 --> 00:01:01.159
because you or someone you love has
been through something similar.

00:01:02.839 --> 00:01:07.190
Now in Ian's case,
it was juvenile type I diabetes.

00:01:07.190 --> 00:01:09.630
There's nothing you can
do to prevent type I.

00:01:09.630 --> 00:01:15.120
And once you have it, it affects every
meal you eat, every jog you take,

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:19.370
every night sleep you have for
the rest of your life.

00:01:19.370 --> 00:01:22.500
It dramatically complicates
every other disease you get.

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:24.750
For Ian, at school, getting a cold or

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:27.690
a flu from another kid is
actually a very serious affair.

00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:33.760
And Ian has to check in with the school
nurse four times every single day.

00:01:34.790 --> 00:01:40.740
And if you knew Ian, if you knew him
like I do, then you would be so,

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:46.870
so proud of the amount of courage inside
of that little person every single day.

00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:52.810
Now I'm trained as an economist,
but I love people.

00:01:52.810 --> 00:01:58.351
And if you know any economist, you know
that those two don't always go together.

00:01:58.351 --> 00:02:02.481
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Dean Levon in the back's

00:02:02.481 --> 00:02:04.247
laughing a bit.

00:02:04.247 --> 00:02:08.010
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So for me,

00:02:08.010 --> 00:02:13.050
Ian raises a very important question,
which is, as a society,

00:02:13.050 --> 00:02:17.765
what are the binding constraints
keeping us from cures,

00:02:17.765 --> 00:02:23.620
from making the deadly manageable and
making the manageable curable?

00:02:23.620 --> 00:02:25.430
And I think the answer may surprise you.

00:02:25.430 --> 00:02:30.870
See, I find that many of us imagine where
cures come from to be something like this.

00:02:30.870 --> 00:02:34.884
Some scientist with gray hair
somewhere with a chemistry said,

00:02:34.884 --> 00:02:37.274
maybe even something a bit like that.

00:02:37.274 --> 00:02:38.195
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

00:02:38.195 --> 00:02:40.070
I think I know where we get that from.

00:02:40.070 --> 00:02:44.428
I mean, this is a real photo,
one of medicine's greatest hits,

00:02:44.428 --> 00:02:48.960
the accidental discovery of
penicillin by Sir Alexander Fleming.

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:54.448
But there's one problem, that was in 1928.

00:02:54.448 --> 00:02:57.970
[LAUGH] Do you think anything's changed,
perhaps, since then?

00:02:57.970 --> 00:03:02.800
Yes, today, we have a process,
it's a process we trust.

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:07.249
And it is long, it is regulated,
it is complicated and

00:03:07.249 --> 00:03:13.369
it moves science into clinical trials and
clinical trials into medicine.

00:03:13.369 --> 00:03:17.929
And if we want this process to work,
if we want the scientists to have

00:03:17.929 --> 00:03:22.409
the data that they need,
if we want clinical trials to run on time,

00:03:22.409 --> 00:03:26.489
if we want patients to have safe and
effective medication and

00:03:26.489 --> 00:03:32.430
to be notified when there's a cure for
them, then we need a big, big idea.

00:03:32.430 --> 00:03:36.660
We need connected health intelligence.

00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:37.580
Let me give you an example.

00:03:37.580 --> 00:03:42.010
So a couple of weeks ago,
I was reading a blog post from Bill Gates,

00:03:42.010 --> 00:03:46.780
he was writing about Alzheimer's disease,
no cure today for Alzheimer's.

00:03:46.780 --> 00:03:50.790
And he wrote the number one barrier
to a breakthrough in Alzheimer's,

00:03:50.790 --> 00:03:56.560
number one, is a more efficient
way to recruit patients for

00:03:56.560 --> 00:04:00.420
clinical trials earlier in
the diseases progression.

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:04.280
They need to find the right
patient at the right time.

00:04:04.280 --> 00:04:06.950
And this isn't just a problem for
Alzheimer's.

00:04:06.950 --> 00:04:10.714
Today, we're running ten times more
clinical trials than we were in the early

00:04:10.714 --> 00:04:11.350
2000s.

00:04:11.350 --> 00:04:16.393
That's a huge increase in
our ability to find cures,

00:04:16.393 --> 00:04:23.579
but over 80% of clinical trials can't
recruit enough patients on time.

00:04:23.579 --> 00:04:26.950
So what's keeping us from
solving this matching problem?

00:04:26.950 --> 00:04:29.840
We solve matching problems all day,
every day.

00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:32.110
We solve it in ride hailing,

00:04:32.110 --> 00:04:36.870
we solve it in home sharing,
in song suggestions, in matchmaking.

00:04:36.870 --> 00:04:41.080
It's not even that hard to imagine
some sort of Google search

00:04:41.080 --> 00:04:46.450
across all health information,
but it doesn't exist.

00:04:46.450 --> 00:04:48.570
We don't have connected
health intelligence.

00:04:49.830 --> 00:04:54.508
It's not for lack of data,
healthcare is generating more

00:04:54.508 --> 00:04:58.529
data than any other industry
in the entire world.

00:05:00.829 --> 00:05:02.400
So what is it?

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:07.381
What is it that is keeping
the researchers trying to find cures,

00:05:07.381 --> 00:05:12.733
the doctors trying to prevent
diseases earlier in the progression,

00:05:12.733 --> 00:05:17.271
the people who are trying to
save your life from that data?

00:05:17.271 --> 00:05:22.680
It's killing people, and
it is a big, big problem.

00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:23.590
So what are we going to do?

00:05:25.410 --> 00:05:29.290
Well, the good news, and there is good
news, everyone, there is good news,

00:05:29.290 --> 00:05:33.510
is that the three major barriers,
the three major

00:05:33.510 --> 00:05:37.330
barriers to connected health
intelligence have now been overcome.

00:05:37.330 --> 00:05:39.041
So I'm going to walk you
through these three.

00:05:39.041 --> 00:05:42.909
And I think by the end you'll agree that
the future of healthcare is going to look

00:05:42.909 --> 00:05:44.709
very different than it does today.

00:05:44.709 --> 00:05:48.870
Those barriers were one,
making health information digital.

00:05:48.870 --> 00:05:51.350
Two was structuring the information.

00:05:51.350 --> 00:05:56.110
And three, the hardest of all,
was preserving patient privacy.

00:05:56.110 --> 00:05:57.260
So let's walk through these.

00:05:57.260 --> 00:06:00.440
Number one,
I want you to imagine the clipboard.

00:06:00.440 --> 00:06:03.934
The clipboard,
the enemy of all progress in healthcare,

00:06:03.934 --> 00:06:06.369
second only perhaps to the fax machine.

00:06:06.369 --> 00:06:09.000
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; You know what I'm talking about.

00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:13.929
All of those papers with the grimy little
pen that everyone else has coughed on.

00:06:13.929 --> 00:06:16.651
And the chain, as though you'd
want to take that pen home.

00:06:16.651 --> 00:06:17.904
You know what I'm talking about.

00:06:17.904 --> 00:06:20.989
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; That's going away.

00:06:20.989 --> 00:06:23.949
Now your health information is digital.

00:06:23.949 --> 00:06:27.631
This really started accelerating
about ten years ago, and

00:06:27.631 --> 00:06:32.430
today 98% of hospitals are using some
form of electronic health records.

00:06:32.430 --> 00:06:36.450
So that brings us to number two,
health information is digital, but

00:06:36.450 --> 00:06:38.270
it's messy and unstructured.

00:06:38.270 --> 00:06:40.630
You can kind of imagine
it like millions and

00:06:40.630 --> 00:06:45.260
millions of free text,
doctors' notes in Word documents.

00:06:45.260 --> 00:06:46.910
But in order to make sense of it,

00:06:46.910 --> 00:06:52.020
we need it to look more like an Excel
spreadsheet with nice rows and columns.

00:06:52.020 --> 00:06:53.510
So what happened?

00:06:53.510 --> 00:06:58.170
Well, it's the same technology that
powers your Siri and your Alexa,

00:06:58.170 --> 00:07:03.210
it's a branch of artificial intelligence
called natural language processing.

00:07:03.210 --> 00:07:07.060
And it's the way that computers
are able to turn text into meaning.

00:07:07.060 --> 00:07:10.510
And now, it is so
good that we can scan those millions and

00:07:10.510 --> 00:07:14.480
millions of documents, and
highly technical documents, and

00:07:14.480 --> 00:07:18.065
get structured information out of it,
which is just Incredible.

00:07:18.065 --> 00:07:21.770
But that brings us to three, and
this has a lot to do with you and

00:07:21.770 --> 00:07:24.790
me, this is patient privacy.

00:07:24.790 --> 00:07:28.890
Your health records belong to you and
mine belong to me.

00:07:28.890 --> 00:07:36.100
They do not belong to any insurance
company, any hospital, any researcher.

00:07:36.100 --> 00:07:40.641
And as a society,
we've chosen to value patient

00:07:40.641 --> 00:07:45.754
privacy above researcher's need for
transparency.

00:07:45.754 --> 00:07:48.450
So what happened?

00:07:48.450 --> 00:07:53.725
Well, just in the past few months,
there have been breakthroughs

00:07:53.725 --> 00:07:58.070
in decentralized private
artificial intelligence.

00:07:58.070 --> 00:08:02.580
And in fact, the first paper that's
going to be published on this,

00:08:02.580 --> 00:08:07.990
it's coming out in two days here at
Stanford with applications in medicine.

00:08:07.990 --> 00:08:09.880
So you're now all ahead of the curve.

00:08:11.770 --> 00:08:16.650
This gives researchers the ability to ask
questions and query information that they

00:08:16.650 --> 00:08:22.040
don't otherwise have access to and
is distributed due to privacy concerns.

00:08:22.040 --> 00:08:25.858
So now, with these building blocks,
we have all three things we need for

00:08:25.858 --> 00:08:27.680
connected health intelligence.

00:08:27.680 --> 00:08:31.508
And I think that it's going to
change life for you and

00:08:31.508 --> 00:08:33.890
for me and for people like Ian.

00:08:37.010 --> 00:08:40.280
One of the reasons why I relate so

00:08:40.280 --> 00:08:46.690
much to Ian's story is that in many ways,
it's my own story.

00:08:46.690 --> 00:08:53.066
When I was four years old,
my parents, Ian's grandparents,

00:08:53.066 --> 00:08:58.137
heard the words, for
your son, there is no cure.

00:08:58.137 --> 00:09:03.400
He will live with a life-threatening
autoimmune disorder.

00:09:03.400 --> 00:09:12.070
And I know personally what it would've
meant as a kid to hear, we have a cure.

00:09:12.070 --> 00:09:15.008
To avoid those painful
moments of uncertainty,

00:09:15.008 --> 00:09:18.669
those close calls where I wasn't
sure if I was going to make it.

00:09:18.669 --> 00:09:20.419
If I was going to be an adult one day.

00:09:20.419 --> 00:09:25.441
If I would be here standing
in front of you right now.

00:09:25.441 --> 00:09:29.100
But being sick has a way
of really clarifying for

00:09:29.100 --> 00:09:31.763
you what matters the most and why.

00:09:34.940 --> 00:09:40.240
And I think that the words that my
kids will hear will be different.

00:09:40.240 --> 00:09:49.040
I think the words that my kids will
hear will be yes, yes, we have a cure.

00:09:49.040 --> 00:09:54.147
Thank you.
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:09:54.147 --> 00:09:57.563
[MUSIC]

